To prevent your planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system and adjust the planet's positions accordingly. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function with collision detection:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const distance = planet.position.reduce((a, b) => Math.sqrt(a - b), Infinity);
      if (distance < minDistance) {
        continue;
      }
    }

    planets.push(planet);
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This function checks if the new planet is too close to any existing planet before adding it to the array. If it is too close, it continues to generate a new planet until one is found that meets the distance requirement.

